  "overview": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "United States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "5d7b378961710d3d58b265728a75ec831994cd9964f36d24fe9784560ac50779",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-5d7b37896171-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States, united States v. Alphonso Vonn is a criminal case concerning the validity of a guilty plea entered without proper advisement of the right to assistance of counsel under Rule\u202f11(c)(3)."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Government",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "5d7b378961710d3d58b265728a75ec831994cd9964f36d24fe9784560ac50779",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-5d7b37896171-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Government, the case also raises the question of whether errors in the plea colloquy are reviewed under the harmless error doctrine (Rule\u202f11(h)) or the plain error rule (Rule\u202f52(b))."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "5d7b378961710d3d58b265728a75ec831994cd9964f36d24fe9784560ac50779",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-5d7b37896171-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States, the appellate court set aside the guilty plea, finding the district court failed to advise the right to assistance of counsel as required by Rule\u202f11(c)(3)."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "United States",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "5d7b378961710d3d58b265728a75ec831994cd9964f36d24fe9784560ac50779",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-5d7b37896171-0000-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to United States, the appellate court erred in setting aside the guilty plea; the district court's failure to advise the right to assistance of counsel under Rule\u202f11(c)(3) should not automatically reverse the conviction."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Government",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "5d7b378961710d3d58b265728a75ec831994cd9964f36d24fe9784560ac50779",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-5d7b37896171-0001-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 18,
                "start": 18
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Government, the drafters of the rule intended to negate a prior holding that suggested automatic reversal for a Rule\u202f11 violation."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Government",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "5d7b378961710d3d58b265728a75ec831994cd9964f36d24fe9784560ac50779",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-5d7b37896171-0001-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 19,
                "start": 19
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Government, there is a general presumption that preserved errors are reviewed under the harmless error doctrine (Rule\u202f11(h)), while errors not preserved at trial are reviewed under the plain error rule (Rule\u202f52(b))."
        }
      ],
